1 DEBUG

   The govax debugger examines and controls the emulated VAX and the
   program running on it.  It is modeled on the VMS debugger, and its
   commands follow that debugger's syntax; the commands about the
   machine itself (SHOW TB, SET PTE, ASM, ...) are govax's own.

   Type HELP and a command for its description, and a subtopic for more
   (HELP SET BREAK, HELP EXAMINE /INSTRUCTION).  At a Topic? or
   Subtopic? prompt, type a topic, ? to see the list again, or Return to
   go up a level.

   The VMS command line (DIRECTORY, MACRO, LINK, RUN, ...) isn't
   available at the DBG> prompt: EXIT returns to the console, where it
   is.

2 Built_in_Symbols

   Names the debugger gives values of its own.  Each may be used in an
   expression (see Expressions).

     . or %CURLOC     The current location: the one EXAMINE, DEPOSIT, or
                      EVALUATE/ADDRESS last referred to.
     %NEXTLOC         The location after it.  EXAMINE alone shows it.
     ^ or %PREVLOC    The location before it.
     \ or %CURVAL     The value EXAMINE or EVALUATE last showed, or
                      DEPOSIT last stored.
     %LINE n          The first instruction of source line n.
     %NAME 'string'   The symbol the string names, which may hold
                      characters a name can't (%NAME 'P.AAA').
     %HEX %DEC        Read the numbers of the operand that follows in
     %OCT %BIN        that radix: %HEX 10 is 16, %DEC (10+10) is 20.
     %EXC_NUMBER      The condition value of the exception the program
                      last stopped for, and %EXC_SEVERITY its severity.
     %EXC_NAME        That exception's message name ("ACCVIO") and
     %EXC_FACILITY    facility ("SYSTEM"): strings, which EVALUATE shows
                      and a WHEN clause may compare.
     %CURRENT_SCOPE_ENTRY, %NEXT_SCOPE_ENTRY, %PREVIOUS_SCOPE_ENTRY
                      Call levels of the default scope list (see SET
                      SCOPE).
     %DECWINDOWS      0: govax has only the command interface.

2 Eventpoints

   The debugger has three kinds of eventpoint:

     Breakpoints    Stop the program before an instruction runs (SET
                    BREAK): at an address, at each instruction of a
                    class (/CALL, /BRANCH, /RETURN, /LINE,
                    /INSTRUCTION), when a condition is signaled
                    (/EXCEPTION), or, govax's own, when a machine fault
                    is about to be delivered (/FAULT).
     Tracepoints    Report where the program is, and let it go on (SET
                    TRACE).
     Watchpoints    Stop after the instruction that changes a location
                    (SET WATCH).

   Each may have /AFTER:n (it acts from the nth time on), /TEMPORARY (it
   goes away after it first acts), a WHEN clause (it acts only when the
   condition is true), and a DO clause (commands run when it acts).
   The report names the place by the program's debug symbols:

     break at DBGDIS\START\%LINE 42

   SHOW BREAK, SHOW TRACE, and SHOW WATCH list them, and CANCEL BREAK,
   CANCEL TRACE, and CANCEL WATCH take them away.

2 Expressions

   An address or a value is an expression, which may hold:

     numbers        In the input radix (see Radix); ^X, ^D, ^O, and ^B
                    give a number's radix, as in MACRO (^D35).
     names          A routine, label, or datum from the program's debug
                    symbols, or a symbol of the machine's symbol table
                    (the ones ASM defines, and the system's).
     path names     MODULE\ROUTINE\LABEL (see Path_Names).
     lines          %LINE n, or MODULE\%LINE n: a source line's code.
     registers      R0 to R11, AP, FP, SP, PC, and PSL (or %R0, ...), as
                    their values (see Registers).
     .name          The contents of an address: .PC, .R1+4.
     operators      + - * / MOD, unary + and -, @ (a shift), the
                    comparisons EQL NEQ LSS LEQ GTR GEQ, and AND, OR,
                    XOR, and NOT, with parentheses.
     built-ins      %LINE, %CURLOC, %EXC_NAME, ... (see Built_in_Symbols).

   A range is start:end (EXAMINE A:B), and a list is separated by commas
   (EXAMINE A,B).  A routine's name, in SET BREAK, stands for the address
   after its entry mask.

2 FORTRAN_Programs

   A FORTRAN program compiled /DEBUG has its variables known by what
   they are, though the debugger's language stays MACRO:

     EXAMINE X          In a routine, that routine's X, shown by its type.
     EXAMINE UNIT.X     A unit's X, from anywhere; a dummy argument needs
                        an active call.
     EXAMINE M(2,3)     An element, by FORTRAN's bounds and order;
                        EXAMINE M shows them all.
     EXAMINE S(2:4)     A substring.
     DEPOSIT X = 1.5    A value read as the variable's type.
     EVALUATE X         What it holds.
     SET BREAK UNIT.$LINE30
                        The statement on line 30 ($LINE30 in that
                        routine).

   A stop shows the FORTRAN line above the MACRO line, and STEP goes a
   FORTRAN line at a time (STEP/INSTRUCTION an instruction).  Subscripts
   are read in the input radix, so SET RADIX DECIMAL makes them
   FORTRAN's numbers.  Compile /NOOPTIMIZE for debugging: under
   /OPTIMIZE a variable may be in a register, and EXAMINE says so when
   it knows.  A recursive routine's variables are its frame's: SET SCOPE
   picks the call level.

2 Path_Names

   A name may be qualified by the module and routine it belongs to,
   separated by backslashes: DBGDIS\START\LOOP is the label LOOP in the
   routine START of the module DBGDIS, and DBGDIS\%LINE 42 is line 42 of
   that module.  A path from a routine (START\LOOP) is looked for in each
   module.  \NAME alone is the global symbol NAME.

   A name may also carry its own scope, a call level or a routine's
   activation: 1\R2 is level 1's R2, and REC 1\%R2 is R2 in the second
   activation of REC from the innermost (see SET SCOPE).

   In MACRO a period inside a name is part of it (T.I is one name); one
   that starts an operand is the contents of what follows (.T.I).  SET
   LANGUAGE FORTRAN makes a period select a record's component instead.

2 PSL

   The processor status longword: the CPU's condition codes, trap
   enables, and state.  SHOW PSL and SHOW REGISTERS show it as a value
   and as its fields, and SET PSL changes them:

     C      Carry.
     V      Overflow.
     Z      Zero.
     N      Negative.
     T      Trace: a trace fault comes before the next instruction.
     IV     Integer overflow traps are enabled.
     FU     Floating underflow faults are enabled.
     DV     Decimal overflow traps are enabled.
     IPL    The interrupt priority level, 0 to 31.
     PRV    The previous access mode.
     CUR    The current access mode: kernel, executive, supervisor, or
            user.
     IS     The CPU is on the interrupt stack.
     FPD    First part done: an interrupted instruction resumes.
     TP     Trace pending.
     CM     Compatibility mode (always 0 in govax).

2 Radix

   The debugger reads numbers, and shows them, in a radix: hexadecimal
   unless SET RADIX or the program's language says otherwise (a
   FORTRAN program's is decimal).  The input radix is the one numbers
   typed are read in; the output radix is the one values are shown in.
   SET RADIX sets both, or one with /INPUT or /OUTPUT; SHOW RADIX shows
   them, and CANCEL RADIX restores the language's.  The console has a
   radix of its own, and the two don't affect each other.

2 Registers

   The registers the debugger knows by name:

     R0 to R11, AP (R12), FP (R13), SP (R14), PC (R15)
                    The general registers.
     PSL            The processor status longword (see PSL).

   and the privileged registers: P0BR, P0LR, P1BR, P1LR, SBR, SLR, PCBB,
   SCBB, IPL, ASTLVL, SIRR, SISR, ICCS, NICR, ICR, TODR, RXCS, RXDB,
   TXCS, TXDB, TBDR, SAVISP, SAVPC, SAVPSL, WCSA, WCSB, MAPEN, TBIA,
   TBIS, PMR, SID, and TBCHK.  EXAMINE and DEPOSIT reach the general
   registers and the PSL; SHOW and a register's name (SHOW R3, SHOW
   P0BR) shows any of them.  The registers are the visible process's
   (SET PROCESS), as the scope gives them (SET SCOPE).

2 @_(Execute_Procedure)

   Runs the debugger commands in a file.

   Format

     @filespec

3 Parameter

 filespec

   The file of commands, found as DCL finds a command procedure: a
   volume file, or a host file.  A name without a type gets .COM.

3 Description

   The file is read a line at a time, each line a command, as if typed
   at the DBG> prompt.  A command that fails is reported, and the file
   goes on.  With SET OUTPUT VERIFY each line is echoed after a blank
   as it runs (not at all if it fails before any output), between
   %DEBUG-I-VERIFYICF's "entering" and "exiting" lines.  A command that
   reads lines of its own, such as HELP answering its prompts, reads
   them from the file.  If a command in the file ends the session
   (EXIT), the lines after it are the console's commands.

   When RUN starts the debugger on an image and the logical name
   DBG$INPUT names a file, the debugger reads its commands from that
   file instead of the terminal, to its end, which is EXIT.  A command
   procedure gives the debugger its commands so, as on VMS:

     $ DEFINE/USER_MODE DBG$INPUT SESSION.DBG
     $ RUN/DEBUG PROG

   INCLUDE is another name for @.

3 Example

   1.DBG> @SETUP

     Runs the commands in SETUP.COM.

2 ASM

   Assembles VAX source into the machine's memory, from a file or, with
   no file, one statement at a time at the ASM> prompt.  ASSEMBLE is the
   same command.

   Format

     ASM [filespec]

3 Parameter

 filespec

   The file to assemble.  A VMS file specification
   (SYS$SOURCES:KERNEL.ASM) is a file on a mounted volume, a host path
   ("./my.asm") a host file, and a bare name (MY.ASM) a file in the
   default directory when SET DEFAULT is on a volume.  A bare name that
   isn't there, or any name when the default is on the host, is looked
   for in the host's current directory, the --path directories, and
   govax's built-in files (kernel.asm, ssdef.asm).  Quote a host file's
   name to keep its case.

   With no file, the prompt becomes ASM> and statements are typed, one
   a line, until .END.

3 Description

   The assembler takes VAX MACRO's syntax for instructions, operands,
   labels, and expressions, and a set of directives, some MACRO's and
   some its own.  It writes what it assembles straight into memory, at
   the current location (see Directives BASE and REGION), and keeps its
   symbols in the machine's symbol table, where the debugger's
   expressions find them.  Forward references are allowed, and are
   filled in when the symbol is defined.

   A statement whose last character before any comment is a hyphen
   continues on the next line.  Assembly ends at the file's end or at
   .END; an .END that names an entry address calls it at once.  A
   .INCLUDE of a bare name looks beside the file being assembled first.

   This is not the MACRO command, which makes object modules for LINK.

3 Addressing

   Operands use VAX MACRO's addressing mode syntax:

     Rn                  Register
     (Rn)                Register deferred
     (Rn)+  -(Rn)        Autoincrement, autodecrement
     @(Rn)+              Autoincrement deferred
     #value              Short literal, or immediate if it won't fit
     S^#value  I^#value  Short literal, immediate
     disp(Rn)  @disp(Rn) Displacement [deferred]
     B^ W^ L^disp(Rn)    Displacement of a given size
     address   @address  Relative [deferred]: PC plus a displacement
     B^ W^ L^address     Relative, with a displacement of a given size
     @#address           Absolute
     operand[Rx]         Indexed

   With no B^, W^, or L^, a displacement or relative address whose value
   is already known takes the smallest displacement that holds it; a
   forward reference takes a longword.  A relative operand is stored as
   the distance from the end of the operand to the address, so code that
   uses one must run where it was assembled, or be moved as a whole.

3 Expressions

   Expressions follow VAX MACRO.  A number is decimal unless a radix
   operator says otherwise, and every binary operator has the same
   priority, applied left to right: 1+2*3 is 9, and 1+<2*3> is 7.

     <expression>        Grouping; ( ) also works
     + - * /             Arithmetic
     A@B                 A shifted left B bits (right if B is negative)
     A&B  A!B  A\B       AND, inclusive OR, exclusive OR
     -A  +A              Negation
     ^C A                One's complement
     ^D ^X ^O ^B         Decimal, hexadecimal, octal, binary: ^X1F, or
                         ^X<10+20> for a whole expression
     ^A/text/  'text'    ASCII: up to four characters
     ^M<R2,R3>           A register mask
     .                   The current location

   A number must be all digits of its radix: 0FF is an error, where
   ^X0FF is 255.  The debugger's SET RADIX doesn't affect the
   assembler.

   An expression may use a symbol defined later (a forward reference)
   where it adds or subtracts it, or multiplies it by a constant: SYM+8,
   B-A, 2*SYM.  Anything else done to a forward reference is an error.

3 Labels

   A statement may start with a label, which defines a symbol whose value
   is the current location:

     NAME:        An ordinary label.
     NAME::       A label that survives .CLEAR.
     10$:         A local label: decimal digits followed by "$".

   A local label is known only within its local label block, the code
   between two ordinary labels (.ENTRY, .SCOPE, .SHIM, .REGION, and .END
   end a block too), so 10$ can be used again in each block.  Local
   labels aren't kept in the machine's symbol table.

   A symbol can also be given a value by a direct assignment, whose
   expression may not hold forward references:

     NAME = expression
     NAME == expression
     . = expression       Sets the current location.

3 Directives

   A directive is a statement whose name starts with a period.  HELP ASM
   DIRECTIVES and a directive's name, without the period, describes it
   (HELP ASM DIRECTIVES ALIGN).  ASM also takes VAX MACRO's listing
   directives (.TITLE, .IDENT, .SUBTITLE, .SHOW, .LIST, .PAGE, ...),
   which store nothing.  MACRO's directives for object modules (.PSECT,
   .GLOBAL, .TRANSFER, ...) are the MACRO command's alone.

4 ADDRESS

   Stores each address of a list as a longword.

     .ADDRESS  address[,address...]

4 ALIGN

   Advances the current location, if need be, to a multiple of the value
   given: 2, 4, or 8 for a word, longword, or quadword.  The bytes passed
   over aren't written.  (In the MACRO command .ALIGN takes a power of
   two, or BYTE, WORD, LONG, QUAD, or PAGE, as VAX MACRO does.)

     .ALIGN  value

4 ASCIC

   Stores a counted string: a byte holding its length (at most 255),
   then its characters.  See ASCII for how a string is written.

     .ASCIC  "string"

4 ASCID

   Stores a string descriptor (class S, type T), then the string's
   characters, the descriptor holding their length and address.  See
   ASCII for how a string is written.

     .ASCID  "string"

4 ASCII

   Stores a string's characters.

     .ASCII  "string"

   As in VAX MACRO, the string may be delimited by any printing character
   but a blank, "=", ";", or "<" that doesn't appear in it, and lowercase
   is kept.  Between or after delimited strings, an expression in angle
   brackets stores one byte:

     .ASCII  /Line one/<13><10>

   A backslash is an ordinary character; there are no escapes like \n.

4 ASCIZ

   Stores a string's characters and a zero byte after them.  See ASCII
   for how a string is written.

     .ASCIZ  "string"

4 BASE

   Sets the current location of the current region (see REGION), where
   the next statement is stored: a virtual or physical address, as
   MAPEN says.  The debugger's SET BASE does the same.

     .BASE  address

4 BLKA

   Reserves longwords, each zero.  See BLKB.

     .BLKA  count

4 BLKB

   Reserves bytes, each zero.  .BLKW, .BLKL, .BLKQ, and .BLKO reserve
   words, longwords, quadwords, and octawords; .BLKF and .BLKA are
   longwords, .BLKD and .BLKG quadwords, and .BLKH octawords.

     .BLKB  count

4 BLKD

   Reserves quadwords, each zero.  See BLKB.

     .BLKD  count

4 BLKF

   Reserves longwords, each zero.  See BLKB.

     .BLKF  count

4 BLKG

   Reserves quadwords, each zero.  See BLKB.

     .BLKG  count

4 BLKH

   Reserves octawords, each zero.  See BLKB.

     .BLKH  count

4 BLKL

   Reserves longwords, each zero.  See BLKB.

     .BLKL  count

4 BLKO

   Reserves octawords, each zero.  See BLKB.

     .BLKO  count

4 BLKQ

   Reserves quadwords, each zero.  See BLKB.

     .BLKQ  count

4 BLKW

   Reserves words, each zero.  See BLKB.

     .BLKW  count

4 BYTE

   Stores each value of a list as a byte.  A value may be signed (-1) or
   unsigned (255).  .WORD, .LONG, .QUAD, and .OCTA store words,
   longwords, quadwords, and octawords the same way.  A .QUAD or .OCTA
   value that is a single number (^X0123456789ABCDEF) is stored whole;
   any other expression is computed as a longword and sign-extended.

     .BYTE  value[,value...]

4 CASE

   Stores a CASEB, CASEW, or CASEL instruction's table of displacements,
   which must follow the instruction: each destination's distance from
   the table's start, as a word.  Destinations may be forward
   references.  The table must have exactly limit+1 entries, which
   nothing checks.

     .CASE  destination[,destination...]

   For example, a selector of 0 to 2 in R0:

     CASEB   R0, #0, #2
     .CASE   RED, BLUE, GREEN
     MOVL    #ERR, R0          ; out of range

4 CLEAR

   Removes a symbol from the machine's symbol table, or with no name
   every symbol but those defined by a NAME:: label.

     .CLEAR  [name]

4 CONSOLE

   Accepted, and ignored: the rest of the line was a console command in
   the assembler govax's came from.

     .CONSOLE  command

4 D_FLOAT

   Stores each value of a list as a D_floating number (8 bytes).
   .D_FLOATING and .DOUBLE are the same.  See F_FLOAT.

     .D_FLOAT  value[,value...]

4 D_FLOATING

   The same as .D_FLOAT.  See F_FLOAT.

     .D_FLOATING  value[,value...]

4 DOUBLE

   The same as .D_FLOAT.  See F_FLOAT.

     .DOUBLE  value[,value...]

4 END

   Ends the assembly.  At the ASM> prompt, it returns to DBG>.  An entry
   address, if one is given, is recorded as the symbol __ENTRY and called
   (as CALL does) once the assembly ends.

     .END  [entry]

4 ENDC

   Ends an .IF block.  See IF.

     .ENDC

4 ENDM

   Ends a macro definition.  See MACRO.

     .ENDM  [name]

4 ENDR

   Ends a repeat block.  See IRP.

     .ENDR

4 ENTRY

   Defines a procedure's entry point: a label, flagged as an entry, and
   the entry mask word (zero if no mask is given).  CALL and CALLS reach
   it by its name.

     .ENTRY  name[,mask]

   The mask is usually written ^M<R2,R3>.

4 ERROR

   Reports an error of the program's own: the value of the expression,
   if there is one and it isn't zero, then the statement's comment.
   .WARN reports a warning the same way.  They're for checking a
   macro's arguments, in a conditional block.

     .ERROR  [expression] ;comment
     .IIF    BLANK <ARG>, .ERROR ; ARG is required

4 F_FLOAT

   Stores each value of a list as an F_floating number (4 bytes).
   .F_FLOATING and .FLOAT are the same; .D_FLOAT, .G_FLOATING, and
   .H_FLOATING store 8, 8, and 16 bytes.  A value is a decimal number
   (1.5, -0.1, 6.02E23), rounded once to the format, half away from
   zero, as the VAX rounds.  One too large for the format is an error,
   and one too small is stored as zero.

     .F_FLOAT  value[,value...]

4 F_FLOATING

   The same as .F_FLOAT.  See F_FLOAT.

     .F_FLOATING  value[,value...]

4 FAB

   Builds an RMS file access block (80 bytes) at the current location,
   as VAX MACRO's $FAB macro does, its fields from keywords: .FAB
   FNM=<NAME.DAT>, FAC=GET, ...  .RAB builds a record access block (68
   bytes) the same way, as $RAB does.  .RMSDEF defines the FAB$, RAB$,
   and RMS$ symbols.

     .FAB  [keyword=value[,keyword=value...]]

4 FLOAT

   The same as .F_FLOAT.  See F_FLOAT.

     .FLOAT  value[,value...]

4 G_FLOATING

   Stores each value of a list as a G_floating number (8 bytes).  See
   F_FLOAT.

     .G_FLOATING  value[,value...]

4 H_FLOATING

   Stores each value of a list as an H_floating number (16 bytes).  See
   F_FLOAT.

     .H_FLOATING  value[,value...]

4 IF

   Conditional assembly, as in VAX MACRO: the statements up to the
   matching .ENDC are assembled only if the condition is met.  Blocks
   nest up to 31 deep.  Statements left out, their labels too, aren't
   assembled at all.

     .IF    condition argument
     ...
     .ENDC

   Within a block, .IF_FALSE (.IFF), .IF_TRUE (.IFT), and
   .IF_TRUE_FALSE (.IFTF) assemble what follows if the condition wasn't
   met, was met, or either way.  The conditions, each with a short form:

     EQUAL EQ, NOT_EQUAL NE          The expression is 0, or isn't.
     GREATER GT, LESS_EQUAL LE       It's above 0, or isn't.
     LESS_THAN LT, GREATER_EQUAL GE  It's below 0, or isn't.
     DEFINED DF, NOT_DEFINED NDF     The symbol is defined, or isn't
                                     (A&B needs both, A!B either).
     BLANK B, NOT_BLANK NB           The text is blank, or isn't.
     IDENTICAL IDN, DIFFERENT DIF    Two texts are the same, or differ.

   An expression may not use a symbol defined later.

     .IF     DF DEBUG
     MOVL    #1, R0
     .IF_FALSE
     CLRL    R0
     .ENDC

4 IF_FALSE

   In an .IF block, assembles what follows if the condition wasn't met.
   .IFF is the same.  See IF.

     .IF_FALSE

4 IF_TRUE

   In an .IF block, assembles what follows if the condition was met.  .IFT
   is the same.  See IF.

     .IF_TRUE

4 IF_TRUE_FALSE

   In an .IF block, assembles what follows either way.  .IFTF is the same.
   See IF.

     .IF_TRUE_FALSE

4 IIF

   Assembles one statement if a condition is met: .IF's conditions.

     .IIF  condition argument, statement
     .IIF  NOT_DEFINED SS$_NORMAL, .INCLUDE "ssdef.asm"

4 INCLUDE

   Assembles another file's statements in place of this one, then goes
   on.  A bare name is looked for beside the file being assembled first.

     .INCLUDE  "filespec"

4 IRP

   Repeat blocks, as in VAX MACRO: the statements up to .ENDR are
   assembled several times.

     .REPEAT  count            count times (.REPT is the same)
     .IRP     symbol,<a,b,...> once for each argument, symbol replaced
                               by it
     .IRPC    symbol,<text>    once for each character
     ...
     .ENDR

   .MEXIT in a repeat block ends it, skipping the repetitions left.

     .IRP    REG, <R2, R3, R4>
     CLRL    REG
     .ENDR

4 IRPC

   Repeats a block once for each character of a text.  See IRP.

     .IRPC  symbol,<text>

4 JEQL

   A conditional jump of any distance: an inverted short branch around a
   JMP.  .JEQLU is the same, and .JNEQ and .JNEQU jump when not equal.

     .JEQL  destination

4 LONG

   Stores each value of a list as a longword.  See BYTE.

     .LONG  value[,value...]

4 MACRO

   Macros, as in VAX MACRO.  A definition is a named block of source; a
   statement naming the macro assembles the block with the call's
   arguments in place of the formal ones.  Macros are tried before
   instructions, so a macro can replace one.

     .MACRO  name [formal[=default],...]
     ...
     .ENDM   [name]

     name    [argument,...]

   A formal argument named ?NAME gets a created local label (30000$,
   ...) when the call gives it no argument.  A call's arguments are
   positional, or formal=value in any order; <...> or ^x...x encloses one
   holding commas or blanks, and \symbol passes a symbol's value in
   decimal.  An argument goes in everywhere in the block, strings and
   comments too; an apostrophe next to a formal's name joins it to the
   text around it (PREFIX'_'N).  In a macro's lines %LENGTH(text),
   %LOCATE(text1,text2[,start]), and %EXTRACT(start,length,text) are
   replaced by their results.  The other macro directives:

     .MEXIT                   Ends the expansion early.
     .MDELETE name,...        Deletes definitions.
     .NARG    symbol          The number of positional arguments given.
     .NCHR    symbol,text     The number of characters in the text.
     .NTYPE   symbol,operand  The operand's addressing mode.

   ASM uses only the macros its source defines; the MACRO command also
   finds them in macro libraries.

4 MASK

   Stores a register mask as a word.

     .MASK  ^M<R2,R3>

4 MDELETE

   Deletes macro definitions.  See MACRO.

     .MDELETE  name[,name...]

4 MEXIT

   Ends a macro's expansion, or a repeat block, early.  See MACRO.

     .MEXIT

4 NARG

   Sets a symbol to the number of positional arguments a macro call gave.
   See MACRO.

     .NARG  symbol

4 NCHR

   Sets a symbol to the number of characters in a text.  See MACRO.

     .NCHR  symbol,text

4 NTYPE

   Sets a symbol to an operand's addressing mode.  See MACRO.

     .NTYPE  symbol,operand

4 OCTA

   Stores each value of a list as an octaword.  See BYTE.

     .OCTA  value[,value...]

4 PACKED

   Stores a packed decimal string: two digits a byte, most significant
   first, the sign in the last byte's low half (^XC plus, ^XD minus).
   An even number of digits gets a zero first half byte.

     .PACKED  number[,symbol]

   The number has 0 to 31 digits and an optional sign.  The symbol, if
   given, is set to the number of digits, the length the decimal
   instructions take.

4 PRINT

   Shows quoted strings and the values of expressions, in decimal, while
   assembling (nothing after SET NOVERBOSE at the console).

     .PRINT  item[,item...]

4 QUAD

   Stores each value of a list as a quadword.  See BYTE.

     .QUAD  value[,value...]

4 RAB

   Builds an RMS record access block.  See FAB.

     .RAB  [keyword=value[,keyword=value...]]

4 REGION

   Chooses the region statements are stored in: P0 (also 0 or PROCESS)
   or S0 (2 or SYSTEM).  The assembler keeps a current location for
   each, which .BASE sets.  After INITIALIZE/MEMORY, P0's starts at ^X200 and S0's
   at the first page after the system's tables.

     .REGION  region

4 REPEAT

   Repeats a block a number of times.  .REPT is the same.  See IRP.

     .REPEAT  count

4 RMSDEF

   Defines the FAB$, RAB$, and RMS$ symbols.  See FAB.

     .RMSDEF

4 SCB

   Puts a handler's address in a vector of the system control block, the
   one SCBB points to.

     .SCB  vector,address[,stack]

   The vector is the slot's offset, a multiple of 4, usually a symbol
   such as EXC$ACCVIO.  The handler must be at a longword boundary.  The
   stack is KSP (the default) or ISP.  (.VECTOR, which did this for the
   code after it, isn't available: it reports NOTLIVE.)

4 SCOPE

   Starts a new local label block at a new name, as .ENTRY does, but
   with no mask and no code of its own.

     .SCOPE  name

4 SET

   Gives a symbol a value.  /PERMANENT keeps it across .CLEAR, and
   /ENTRY and /LABEL flag it as an entry point or a label.

     .SET  [/PERMANENT] [/ENTRY] [/LABEL] name value

4 SHIM

   Makes a shim: the routine a shareable image's entry point leads to
   when an image is activated.  The microkernel's sources are mostly
   these.

     .SHIM  name,[code],image,offset

   An image's reference to image+offset (LIBRTL+^X0478) is bound to the
   routine name.  With a code other than 0, the assembler also makes a
   stub named name that hands the call to govax's own routine with that
   code (an XFC instruction); with 0, name must be a routine in VAX code.
   The image is named without .EXE, and the offset must be the image's
   transfer vector entry exactly.

     .SHIM  LIB$PUT_OUTPUT, 0, LIBRTL, ^X0478

4 SPACE

   Reserves bytes, each set to the fill value (0 if none is given).

     .SPACE  count[,fill]

4 VERSION

   Sets the permanent symbol SYS$MK_VERSION, the microkernel's version,
   to a decimal number.  Nothing is stored.

     .VERSION  number

4 WARN

   Reports a warning of the program's own.  See ERROR.

     .WARN  [expression] ;comment

4 WORD

   Stores each value of a list as a word.  See BYTE.

     .WORD  value[,value...]

2 ASSEMBLE

   The same as ASM.

   Format

     ASSEMBLE [filespec]

2 CALL

   Calls a routine of the program, as a CALLS instruction would, and
   stops when it returns.

   Format

     CALL routine [(argument[,argument...])]

3 Parameters

 routine

   The routine's address: its name, or an expression.

 argument

   The arguments, pushed as CALLS pushes them: expressions, read as the
   console reads them (in the console's radix, not the debugger's).
   The list may follow the routine after a blank.

3 Qualifiers
/BREAK

   The same as /STEP.

/DBG

   The same as /STEP.

/DEBUG

   The same as /STEP.

/STEP

   Stops at the routine's first instruction, after its entry mask,
   rather than running it.

3 Description

   The routine runs until it returns, a breakpoint stops it, or Ctrl/C
   interrupts it.  The value it returns is in R0.

3 Example

   1.DBG> CALL FACT (5)

     Calls FACT with one argument, 5.

2 CANCEL

   Takes away eventpoints, and puts settings back as they were.  CLEAR
   is the same command.

   Format

     CANCEL keyword [qualifiers] [parameter]

3 BREAK

   Takes away breakpoints.  BREAKPOINT is the same keyword.

   Format

     CANCEL BREAK [qualifier] [address[,address...]]

4 Parameter

 address

   Where a breakpoint is: an expression, as SET BREAK was given it.

4 Qualifiers
/ALL

   Every breakpoint.

/BRANCH

   The breakpoint on branch instructions.

/CALL

   The breakpoint on call instructions.

/EXCEPTION

   The breakpoint on exceptions.

/FAULT

      /FAULT[=code]

   govax's breakpoints on machine faults: the one for the fault code, or
   all of them.

/INSTRUCTION

      /INSTRUCTION[=(opcode[,opcode...])]

   The breakpoints on instructions: of the opcodes named, or all of them.

/LINE

   The breakpoint on source lines.

/RETURN

   The breakpoints on returns.

4 Description

   With addresses, the breakpoints there go; with a qualifier, those of
   that kind.  Cancelling when none is set is %DEBUG-I-NOBREAKS.

4 Example

   1.DBG> CANCEL BREAK/ALL

     Takes away every breakpoint.

3 INTERRUPT

   Takes away an interrupt that is waiting to be delivered (govax's
   own).

   Format

     CANCEL INTERRUPT [/ALL] [vector]

4 Parameter

 vector

   The interrupt's offset in the system control block, in hexadecimal.

4 Qualifier
/ALL

   Every interrupt waiting.

3 MODE

   Puts SET MODE's display modes back as they were at the start.

   Format

     CANCEL MODE

3 RADIX

   Puts the input and output radix back to the program's language's:
   decimal for FORTRAN, hexadecimal otherwise.

   Format

     CANCEL RADIX

3 SCOPE

   Goes back to the default scope list (every call level, level 0 the
   current one), ending SET SCOPE's list or SET SCOPE/CURRENT's level.

   Format

     CANCEL SCOPE

3 SOURCE

   Forgets the directories SET SOURCE named, so a module's source file
   is looked for where the image says it is.

   Format

     CANCEL SOURCE

3 STRINGS

   Empties the console's string pool (govax's own; see SHOW
   STRING_POOL).  The descriptors already made from it are no longer
   good, so empty it only when the programs that use them are gone from
   memory.

   Format

     CANCEL STRINGS

3 SYMBOLS

   Removes symbols from the machine's symbol table (govax's own): the
   one ASM defines symbols in, and SHOW SYMBOL/ALL lists.  System symbols
   are never removed.  The console's DELETE/SYMBOL deletes DCL symbols,
   which are another table.

   Format

     CANCEL SYMBOLS name
     CANCEL SYMBOLS/ALL
     CANCEL SYMBOLS/TEMPORARY

4 Qualifiers
/ALL

   Every symbol of the user's.

/TEMPORARY

   Every symbol of the user's not made permanent (a NAME:: label, or
   .SET/PERMANENT).

3 TB

   Empties the translation buffer (govax's own), the cache of recent
   virtual to physical address translations.  The next access to each
   page walks the page tables again.  TRANSLATION_BUFFER is the same
   keyword.

   Format

     CANCEL TB

3 TRACE

   Takes away tracepoints.  TRACEPOINT is the same keyword.

   Format

     CANCEL TRACE [qualifier] [address[,address...]]

4 Qualifiers
/ALL

   Every tracepoint.

/BRANCH

   The tracepoint on branch instructions.

/CALL

   The tracepoint on call instructions.

/INSTRUCTION

      /INSTRUCTION[=(opcode[,opcode...])]

   The tracepoints on instructions: of the opcodes named, or all of
   them.

/LINE

   The tracepoint on source lines.

/RETURN

   The tracepoints on returns.

3 TYPE

   Ends SET TYPE/OVERRIDE's type, so locations are typed by the
   program's debug symbols again.  SET TYPE LONGWORD puts back the type
   of locations no symbol types.

   Format

     CANCEL TYPE/OVERRIDE

4 Qualifier
/OVERRIDE

   The override type.

3 WATCH

   Takes away watchpoints.  WATCHPOINT is the same keyword.

   Format

     CANCEL WATCH [/ALL] [location[,location...]]

4 Qualifier
/ALL

   Every watchpoint.

2 CLEAR

   The same as CANCEL.

   Format

     CLEAR keyword [qualifiers] [parameter]

2 DEPOSIT

   Stores a value at a location: a register, or an address.

   Format

     DEPOSIT[/qualifier] location = value

3 Parameters

 location

   A register (R0 to PC, or PSL) or an address: an expression (see
   Expressions).

 value

   A number in the input radix; a floating value written as a decimal
   number (1.5, -2.5E3); a string in quotation marks or apostrophes; or,
   with /INSTRUCTION, an instruction in quotation marks.

3 Qualifiers
/AC

   The same as /ASCIC.

/AD

   The same as /ASCID.

/ASCIC

   A counted string: a count byte, then the text.

/ASCID

   Text written where the string descriptor at the location points,
   padded with blanks or cut to the descriptor's length.

/ASCII

      /ASCII[:n]

   Text, padded with blanks or cut to n characters, n read in the input
   radix.

/ASCIW

   A string with a count word, then the text.

/ASCIZ

   Text and a zero byte after it.

/AW

   The same as /ASCIW.

/AZ

   The same as /ASCIZ.

/BYTE

   A byte.

/CONDITION_VALUE

   A longword.

/DATE_TIME

   A quadword VMS time, from a date and time ("11-OCT-2026 14:03").

/D_FLOAT

   A D_floating value.

/FLOAT

   An F_floating value.  /F_FLOAT is the same.

/F_FLOAT

   The same as /FLOAT.

/G_FLOAT

   A G_floating value.

/H_FLOAT

   An H_floating value.

/INSTRUCTION

   Assembles the instruction in quotation marks at the location: its
   numbers in the input radix, its names the program's, a branch's
   displacement from the location.  An image's code is written though
   its pages are read-only.  SET TYPE INSTRUCTION makes this the
   default.

/LONGWORD

   A longword.

/OCTAWORD

   An octaword, 16 bytes.

/PACKED

      /PACKED:n

   A packed decimal string of n digits.

/QUADWORD

   A quadword.

/TYPE

      /TYPE=(name)

   A value of the type of the program's datum name.

/WORD

   A word.

3 Description

   The value's type is the qualifier's, else SET TYPE/OVERRIDE's, else
   the type the program's debug symbols give the location, else SET
   TYPE's, else a longword.  A value wider than a longword deposited in
   a register fills the registers after it too: DEPOSIT/D_FLOAT R0 = 1.5
   sets R0 and R1.

   DEPOSIT PSL changes only the processor status longword's low word,
   the PSW (DEPOSIT/BYTE only its low byte); SET PSL changes the rest.
   Setting T (^X10) asks for tracing, but going on from the stop clears
   it again (see STEP).

3 Examples

   1.DBG> DEPOSIT R0 = 5

     Sets R0 to 5.

   2.DBG> DEPOSIT/FLOAT RATE = 2.75

     Stores an F_floating 2.75 at RATE.

   3.DBG> DEPOSIT/ASCIC NAME = 'SMITH'

     Stores a count byte of 5 and SMITH at NAME.

   4.DBG> DEPOSIT/INSTRUCTION .PC = 'MOVL #20A,R0'

     Assembles a MOVL where the program will go next.

2 EVALUATE

   Shows the value of an expression.

   Format

     EVALUATE[/qualifier] expression

3 Parameter

 expression

   An expression (see Expressions).  A datum's name stands for what it
   holds.

3 Qualifiers
/ADDRESS

   Shows a name's address, not what it holds.

/BINARY

   Shows the value in binary.

/CONDITION_VALUE

   Shows the message of the status the value is.

/DECIMAL

   Shows the value in decimal.

/HEXADECIMAL

   Shows the value in hexadecimal.

/OCTAL

   Shows the value in octal.

3 Description

   The value is shown in the output radix unless a radix qualifier is
   given.  EVAL is the same command.

3 Examples

   1.DBG> EVALUATE/DECIMAL R2+10

     Shows R2 plus 10 (in the input radix), in decimal.

   2.DBG> EVALUATE/CONDITION_VALUE R0

     Shows the message of R0's status, as
     %SYSTEM-S-NORMAL, normal successful completion.

2 EXAMINE

   Shows what is at a location: data, or an instruction.

   Format

     EXAMINE[/qualifier...] [location[,location...]]

3 Parameter

 location

   An expression (see Expressions): an address, a name, or a register.
   start:end is a range, each item from start through end.  With no
   location, EXAMINE shows the one after the last one shown.

3 Qualifiers
/AC

   The same as /ASCIC.

/AD

   The same as /ASCID.

/ASCIC

   A count byte, then that much text.

/ASCID

   A string descriptor: the text where it points.

/ASCII

      /ASCII[:n]

   Bytes as ASCII text: n of them, n read in the input radix.

/ASCIW

   A count word, then that much text.

/ASCIZ

   Text up to a zero byte.

/AW

   The same as /ASCIW.

/AZ

   The same as /ASCIZ.

/BINARY

   Integers in binary, for this command.

/BYTE

   Each item a byte.

/CONDITION_VALUE

   A longword as the message of the status it is: %SYSTEM-S-NORMAL,
   normal successful completion.

/CONSTANTS

   With /INSTRUCTION, govax's own: a constant operand named by the
   symbol that defines it (MOVL S^#DBGDIS\LIMIT,R2).

/DATE_TIME

   A quadword as a VMS date and time, 11-OCT-2026 14:03:22.17.

/DECIMAL

   Integers in decimal, for this command.

/DEFAULT

   Integers in the output radix (the default).

/D_FLOAT

   Each item a D_floating value (8 bytes).  See /FLOAT.

/FLOAT

   Each item an F_floating value (4 bytes).  A value from 0.1 up to 10**7
   (10**15 for D and G) is shown in fixed notation, 5350.000, others
   with an exponent, 1.9117807E-38.  /F_FLOAT is the same.

/F_FLOAT

   The same as /FLOAT.

/G_FLOAT

   Each item a G_floating value (8 bytes).  See /FLOAT.

/HEXADECIMAL

   Integers in hexadecimal, for this command.

/H_FLOAT

   Each item an H_floating value (16 bytes).  See /FLOAT.

/INSTRUCTION

   The machine instruction at each location, in the VMS debugger's
   layout, with names from the program's debug symbols:

     DBGDIS\START\%LINE 42:  MOVL     S^#0A,R2

   A range lists every instruction in it.

/LONGWORD

   Each item a longword.

/OCTAL

   Integers in octal, for this command.

/OCTAWORD

   Each item an octaword, 16 bytes.  Type at least /OCTA, since /OCTAL
   starts the same.

/OPERANDS

      /[NO]OPERANDS[=FULL]

   With /INSTRUCTION, explains each operand: its value, and where it is.
   FULL says more.  SET MODE OPERANDS makes it the default.

/PACKED

      /PACKED:n

   n packed decimal digits (n in the input radix): two a byte, the sign
   in the last half byte.

/PSL

   A longword as a processor status longword, field by field.

/PSW

   A word as a processor status word: the PSL's trap enables and
   condition codes.

/PTE

   govax's own: a longword as a page table entry (its valid bit,
   protection, modify bit, owner, and page frame number).

/QUADWORD

   Each item a quadword.

/SHAREABLE

   With /INSTRUCTION, govax's own: a G^ reference named by the
   shareable image's routine it reaches (CALLS #1,G^LIB$PUT_OUTPUT).

/SOURCE

   The source line of the code at each location, under its module's
   name.

/SYMBOLIC

      /[NO]SYMBOLIC

   /NOSYMBOLIC names a location typed as a number by its address
   (00000200: 00000000), not by the symbol there.  SET MODE [NO]SYMBOLIC
   chooses whether an instruction's addresses are names.

/TYPE

      /TYPE=(name)

   Each item typed as the program's datum name is.

/WORD

   Each item a word.

3 Description

   Without /INSTRUCTION, EXAMINE shows data, typed by a type qualifier,
   else by SET TYPE/OVERRIDE's type, else by the program's debug symbols
   where the location names a datum (an array as TABLE[0:3], a string
   through its descriptor, a floating value as a number), else by SET
   TYPE's type (a longword unless it says otherwise).  An integer is
   shown in the output radix.  A type wider than a longword shown from a
   register takes the registers after it too (EXAMINE/D_FLOAT R0 is R0
   and R1).  EX is the same command.

3 Examples

   1.DBG> EXAMINE R0,R1

     Shows R0 and R1.

   2.DBG> EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION .PC:.PC+10

     Shows the instructions from the PC on, for ^X10 bytes.

   3.DBG> EXAMINE/ASCII:10 BUFFER

     Shows ^X10 (16) bytes of BUFFER as text, in the default radix.

2 EXIT

   Ends the debugger session and returns to the console.

   Format

     EXIT

3 Description

   A program still running under the debugger is ended (its exit
   handlers aren't run), and RUN's status is %X10028009; one that has
   exited leaves its exit status.  A log being written (SET OUTPUT LOG)
   is closed.  The end of DBG$INPUT's file ends the session as EXIT
   does.

2 GO

   Runs the program, from where it is or from an address.

   Format

     GO [address]

3 Parameter

 address

   Where to start: an expression.  Without it the program goes on from
   its PC.

3 Description

   The program runs until it exits, a breakpoint stops it, or Ctrl/C
   interrupts it.  A breakpoint at the address the run starts from
   doesn't stop it at once.  Going on from a break on a condition no
   handler took continues the condition (%DEBUG-I-CONFROMEXC).  G and
   EXECUTE are the same command.

2 HELP

   Shows help on the debugger's commands, from its help library.

   Format

     HELP [topic [subtopic...]]

3 Parameter

 topic

   A command or other topic, and subtopics of it.  A word may be
   abbreviated; * matches any topic and % any character; "..." after
   the first word shows the topic and everything below it.  With no
   topic, HELP shows this library's topics.

3 Description

   The library is the file the logical name DBG$HELP names, or
   SYS$HELP:DBG$HELP.HLB on the system volume, or govax's own copy of it.
   Its one module, DEBUG, holds the commands, so HELP SET BREAK shows
   the library's DEBUG SET BREAK.

   After a topic with subtopics HELP prompts for one, naming the topic
   (DEBUG SET Subtopic?); Return goes up a level, to Topic?, and Return
   there ends HELP.  At Topic? the words are the library's own topics
   (DEBUG).  ? shows the topic again, and Ctrl/Z ends HELP at once.
   When the debugger reads its commands from a file (DBG$INPUT, or @),
   its next lines answer the prompts, so a file must answer them, with
   empty lines to end HELP.

2 INCLUDE

   The same as @: runs the debugger commands in a file.

   Format

     INCLUDE filespec

2 QUIT

   Ends the debugger session and returns to the console, as EXIT does.
   On VMS QUIT skips the program's exit handlers, which EXIT runs; govax
   runs none for either, so the two are the same.

   Format

     QUIT

2 SET

   Changes what the debugger does or shows, or the machine's state.

   Format

     SET keyword [qualifiers] [parameters]

   BREAKPOINT, TRACEPOINT, and WATCHPOINT are the same keywords as
   BREAK, TRACE, and WATCH.

3 BASE

   Sets the current location, where the assembler stores the next
   statement (govax's own; .BASE does the same).

   Format

     SET BASE address

3 BREAK

   Sets breakpoints: the program stops before the instruction runs.

   Format

     SET BREAK [qualifiers] [address[,address...]]
               [WHEN (condition)] [DO (command[;command...])]

4 Parameters

 address

   Where to stop: an expression.  A routine's name stands for its first
   instruction, after the entry mask.  None is given with /CALL,
   /BRANCH, /LINE, /INSTRUCTION, /EXCEPTION, or /FAULT.

 condition

   An expression: the breakpoint stops the program only when it is true
   (not zero).

 command

   Commands run when the breakpoint stops the program.

4 Qualifiers
/AFTER

      /AFTER:n

   Stops from the nth time the breakpoint is reached on.

/BRANCH

   Stops at each branch instruction.

/CALL

   Stops at each call instruction (CALLS, CALLG, JSB, BSBB, BSBW).

/EXCEPTION

   Stops when a condition is signaled, before any handler sees it.

/FAULT

      /FAULT=code

   govax's own: stops when the machine fault with that code
   (hexadecimal, an SCB offset) is about to be delivered.

/INSTRUCTION

      /INSTRUCTION[=(opcode[,opcode...])]

   Stops at each instruction, or each instruction of an opcode named.

/LINE

   Stops at each source line's first instruction.

/RETURN

   Stops at the RET of each call of the routine the address names.

/TEMPORARY

   The breakpoint goes away when it first stops the program.

4 Description

   One class qualifier may be given.  The stop is reported as "break at
   location", "break on lines at ...", and so on (see Eventpoints).  A
   break at the address a run starts from doesn't stop it at once.

4 Examples

   1.DBG> SET BREAK FACT

     Stops at FACT's first instruction.

   2.DBG> SET BREAK/TEMPORARY DBGDIS\START\%LINE 47

     Stops once at line 47 of the module DBGDIS.

   3.DBG> SET BREAK SUB2 WHEN (R0 EQL 5) DO (EXAMINE R0)

     Stops at SUB2 when R0 is 5, and shows R0.

3 FAULT

   Sets how many machine faults SHOW EXCEPTIONS remembers (govax's
   own).  HISTORY is the same keyword.

   Format

     SET FAULT count

3 IMAGE

   Makes an image the current one, as SHOW IMAGE marks it with a *: the
   one whose modules SHOW MODULE lists and SET MODULE/ALL sets.

   Format

     SET IMAGE image

3 LANGUAGE

   Sets the language commands are read in, at the start the main
   program's.

   Format

     SET LANGUAGE language

   The languages are ADA, AMACRO, BASIC, BLISS, C, C_PLUS_PLUS, COBOL,
   DIBOL, FORTRAN, MACRO, MACRO64, PASCAL, PLI, RPG, SCAN, and UNKNOWN.
   The radix becomes the language's: decimal for FORTRAN, hexadecimal
   otherwise.  In MACRO a period inside a name is part of the name; in
   FORTRAN it selects a record's component, and DBG85.X is X in the
   routine DBG85.  A program the FORTRAN compiler made is MACRO, whose
   names (SQUARES.I) are read whole.

3 LOG

   Names the file SET OUTPUT LOG writes the session to: DEBUG.LOG unless
   this names another, with .LOG when the name has no type.  If the
   session is being logged already, the log goes on in the new file.

   Format

     SET LOG filespec

3 MODE

   Sets the display modes, or the CPU's access mode.

   Format

     SET MODE keyword[,keyword...]

   The display modes are the VMS debugger's:

     [NO]SYMBOLIC         Whether an address is shown as a name.
     [NO]OPERANDS[=FULL|BRIEF]
                          Whether EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION explains operands.
     [NO]LINE             Whether the debugger works by source line.
     [NO]G_FLOAT          The floating format.
     [NO]SCROLL, [NO]DYNAMIC, [NO]INTERRUPT
                          Accepted; govax has no screen mode.

   The access modes are govax's own: KERNEL, EXECUTIVE, SUPERVISOR, and
   USER switch the CPU's mode and its stack pointer, and ISP the
   interrupt stack.  SHOW MODE shows the settings, and CANCEL MODE puts
   the display modes back.

3 MODULE

   Marks modules' symbols as set.

   Format

     SET MODULE module[,module...]
     SET MODULE/ALL

   govax reads every module's symbols when it loads an image, so this
   changes only SHOW MODULE's "set" column and which modules a SHOW
   SYMBOL with no IN looks in.

4 Qualifier
/ALL

   Every module of the current image.

3 OUTPUT

   Says where the debugger's output goes, and what is echoed.

   Format

     SET OUTPUT keyword[,keyword...]

   The keywords are:

     [NO]LOG              Whether the session is written to the log
                          file (SET LOG): each command as it was read,
                          and each line of the debugger's output after a
                          "!", so the log can be run with @.  The
                          program's own output isn't in it, nor are
                          HELP's prompts and the answers to them.
     [NO]VERIFY           Whether an @ file's lines are echoed.
     [NO]TERMINAL         Whether the output is shown at the terminal.
     [NO]SCREEN_LOG       Accepted; govax has no screen mode.

   SHOW OUTPUT shows the settings.

3 PAGE

   The same as SET PTE.

   Format

     SET PAGE address [TO address] field=value[,field=value...]

3 PROCESS

   Makes a process the visible one: whose registers and memory EXAMINE,
   DEPOSIT, SHOW REGISTERS, and the rest show.

   Format

     SET PROCESS [/VISIBLE] [pid | name]

4 Parameters

 pid

   A process ID in hexadecimal (302 or 00000302).

 name

   A process name, as SHOW PROCESS lists it.

   With neither, the console's own process (process 1) is visible again.

4 Qualifier
/VISIBLE

   The default, and the only kind: the process is made visible.

4 Description

   The CPU moves to the process, as when a run stops in it.  Running is
   unchanged: STEP and breakpoints belong to process 1, and GO, STEP,
   and CALL give the CPU back to process 1 first.  A process that isn't
   there is %SYSTEM-W-NONEXPR.

3 PSL

   Changes fields of the processor status longword (govax's own).

   Format

     SET PSL field=value[,field=value...]

   The fields are C, V, Z, N, T, IV, FU, DV, IPL, and MOD (or CUR_MOD).
   The values are in the input radix.

     DBG> SET PSL IPL=1F, N=1

3 PTE

   Changes page table entries (govax's own).  PAGE is the same keyword.

   Format

     SET PTE address [TO address] field=value[,field=value...]

   The fields are VALID (V), PROT, MODIFY (M), OWNER (OWN), SOFT (S),
   and PFN (PAGE), the page frame number.  PROT is a number or the name
   SHOW PTE gives (PTE$K_KW).  With TO, each page from the first address
   through the second is changed.

     DBG> SET PTE 200 TO 400 VALID=1,PFN=10

3 QUANTUM

   Sets how many instructions the machine runs between the times it
   looks for interrupts to deliver (govax's own).  0 stops interrupt
   delivery until a quantum is set again.  A fixed quantum makes a run's
   interrupts, and so its timing, the same each time.

   Format

     SET QUANTUM count

3 RADIX

   Sets the radix numbers are typed and shown in.

   Format

     SET RADIX [/INPUT | /OUTPUT] radix

   The radix is DECIMAL, HEXADECIMAL, OCTAL, or BINARY (or 10, 16, 8,
   2).  See Radix.

4 Qualifiers
/INPUT

   Sets only the radix numbers are typed in.

/OUTPUT

   Sets only the radix values are shown in.

3 SCOPE

   Says where names are looked up, and whose registers EXAMINE, DEPOSIT,
   and EVALUATE see.

   Format

     SET SCOPE location[,location...]
     SET SCOPE/MODULE module[,module...]
     SET SCOPE/CURRENT n

4 Parameter

 location

   A call level's number (0 the routine the program is in, 1 its caller,
   and so on out: SHOW CALLS's rows), a routine's or module's path name
   (DBG89\REC, DBG89B), or \, the global scope.

4 Qualifiers
/CURRENT

   Makes level n of the default list (every level) the current one, as
   SET SCOPE n would, until the program goes on.

/MODULE

   Takes each name as a module's.

4 Description

   A name is looked for in each location's module in turn.  The
   registers are the first location's: an outer level's are the ones it
   had when it made its call, from the call frames (R0 and R1 are gone;
   a DEPOSIT to a register a frame saved goes into the frame); a
   routine's are its innermost activation's; a module has none.  The
   list stays until CANCEL SCOPE or another SET SCOPE, and its numbers
   count the levels as they are after each GO or STEP.  SHOW SCOPE shows
   the list.  A name may carry its own scope (see Path_Names).

3 SOURCE

   Names the directories searched for a program's source files when
   they aren't where the image says.

   Format

     SET SOURCE directory[,directory...]

   SHOW SOURCE lists them, and CANCEL SOURCE forgets them.

3 STEP

   Sets what STEP does when it isn't told.

   Format

     SET STEP keyword[,keyword...]

   The keywords are:

     LINE, INSTRUCTION       How far a step goes.
     OVER, INTO (IN), RETURN What to do at a call (RETURN is govax's: run
                             to the routine's return).
     [NO]SILENT              Whether the stop is reported.
     [NO]SOURCE              Whether the source line is shown.

   SHOW STEP shows the settings.

3 TRACE

   Sets tracepoints: the program reports where it is and goes on.

   Format

     SET TRACE [qualifiers] [address[,address...]]
               [WHEN (condition)] [DO (command[;command...])]

   The parameters are SET BREAK's.  The report is "trace at location",
   "trace on lines at ...", and so on.

4 Qualifiers
/AFTER

      /AFTER:n

   Reports from the nth time on.

/BRANCH

   Reports each branch instruction.

/CALL

   Reports each call instruction.

/INSTRUCTION

      /INSTRUCTION[=(opcode[,opcode...])]

   Reports each instruction, or each of an opcode named.

/LINE

   Reports each source line.

/RETURN

   Reports the RET of each call of the routine the address names.

/TEMPORARY

   The tracepoint goes away after its first report.

3 TYPE

   Sets the type EXAMINE and DEPOSIT give a location the program's debug
   symbols don't type, or, with /OVERRIDE, every location.  A qualifier
   on the command wins over both.

   Format

     SET TYPE[/OVERRIDE] keyword

   The keywords are the names of EXAMINE's type qualifiers: BYTE, WORD,
   LONGWORD, QUADWORD, OCTAWORD, FLOAT, D_FLOAT, G_FLOAT, H_FLOAT,
   PACKED:n, ASCII:n (4 characters without n), ASCIC, ASCIW, ASCIZ,
   ASCID (or AC, AW, AZ, AD), DATE_TIME, CONDITION_VALUE, PSL, PSW, and
   TYPE=(name); and INSTRUCTION.  SHOW TYPE names the type; SET TYPE
   LONGWORD puts back the default, and CANCEL TYPE/OVERRIDE ends the
   override.

4 Qualifier
/OVERRIDE

   The type is for every location, whatever the program's symbols say.

3 VM

   Turns address translation on (SET VM) or off (SET NOVM), by the
   privileged register MAPEN (govax's own).  MAPEN is the same keyword.

   Format

     SET [NO]VM

3 WATCH

   Sets watchpoints: the program stops after an instruction that
   changes a location.

   Format

     SET WATCH [qualifiers] location [WHEN (condition)]
               [DO (command[;command...])]

4 Qualifiers
/AFTER

      /AFTER:n

   Stops from the nth change on.

/TEMPORARY

   The watchpoint goes away when it first stops the program.

4 Description

   The location's size comes from the program's debug symbols: a scalar
   by its type, an array (BUFFER[0:15]) item by item, anything else a
   longword.  The report gives the old and new values:

     watch of DBGCMD\COUNT at DBGCMD\START\%LINE 42
        old value: 00000000
        new value: 00000001

2 SHOW

   Shows what the debugger knows, its settings, and the machine's
   state.

   Format

     SHOW keyword [qualifiers] [parameters]

   A register's name as the keyword shows that register, general or
   privileged: SHOW R3, SHOW PC, SHOW P0BR (see Registers).

3 BASE

   Shows the next storage address, where the assembler stores the next
   statement (govax's own).

   Format

     SHOW BASE

3 BREAK

   Lists the breakpoints, with their counts and clauses.  BREAKPOINT and
   BREAKPOINTS are the same keyword.

   Format

     SHOW BREAK

3 CALLS

   Lists the call frames, innermost first, a row each: module, routine,
   line, and the PC relative to the routine and absolute.  A call frame
   is what CALLS and CALLG leave on the stack, linking each routine to
   its caller.  CALL_FRAMES is the same keyword.

   Format

     SHOW CALLS [count]

   With a count, only that many frames.  In a JSB subroutine, which has
   no frame of its own, the row is the routine that holds it.

3 CLOCK

   Shows the interval clock and the time-of-day register (govax's own).

   Format

     SHOW CLOCK

3 CPU_STATUS

   Shows the registers and the PSL, each mode's stack pointer, the
   privileged registers, and the next storage address (govax's own).

   Format

     SHOW CPU_STATUS

3 ESP

   Shows the longwords on the executive mode stack (govax's own).  See
   SP.

   Format

     SHOW ESP [count] [/ALL]

3 EXCEPTIONS

   Shows the machine faults and exceptions recently taken, and the
   interrupts waiting (govax's own).  SET FAULT says how many faults are
   kept.  FAULTS is the same keyword.

   Format

     SHOW EXCEPTIONS

3 IMAGE

   Lists the loaded images, the current one marked with an asterisk:
   whether its symbols are set, and its first and last address.  IMAGES
   is the same keyword.

   Format

     SHOW IMAGE [/FULL]

4 Qualifier
/FULL

      /[NO]FULL

   govax's own: adds each image's counts of modules and routines, the
   file it came from, and its sections in memory: each section's kind
   (read-only, writable, fixup, demand zero, user stack, or a shareable
   image's global section), addresses, size in pages, protection, and
   backing store; then its pages by what they're mapped to now (valid,
   in transition, not yet read, in the page file, demand zero), and the
   pages in memory by where their contents go when their frames are
   reused.  With paging on an image is mapped from its file, so these
   change as the program runs.

3 INSTRUCTIONS

   Lists the VAX instructions and their opcodes (govax's own).  govax
   implements every one.

   Format

     SHOW INSTRUCTIONS [/qualifier] [opcode]

4 Parameter

 opcode

   One instruction's opcode, in hexadecimal: that instruction alone,
   with its operands.

4 Qualifiers
/ALL

   Every instruction with its operands' access and size.

/MODES

   Not available: reports NOMODES.

/PROFILE

   Not available: reports NOPROFILE (govax keeps no count of each
   instruction's executions).

/UNIMPLEMENTED

   The instructions govax doesn't implement, which would be reserved
   instruction faults: none ("0 Instructions.").

3 ISP

   Shows the longwords on the interrupt stack (govax's own).  See SP.

   Format

     SHOW ISP [count] [/ALL]

3 KSP

   Shows the longwords on the kernel mode stack (govax's own).  See SP.

   Format

     SHOW KSP [count] [/ALL]

3 LANGUAGE

   Shows the language commands are read in (see SET LANGUAGE).

   Format

     SHOW LANGUAGE

3 LOG

   Says whether the session is being logged, and to what file.

   Format

     SHOW LOG

3 MEMORY

   Shows the size of physical memory and, once INITIALIZE/MEMORY has
   made page tables, how much of each region (P0, P1, S0) is in use
   (govax's own).  With paging on (INITIALIZE/MEMORY/PAGING), the page
   frames on the free and
   modified lists and in use, the page faults by kind, the modified page
   writer's work, and the page file's use.  VM is the same keyword.

   Format

     SHOW MEMORY

4 Qualifiers
/FULL

      /[NO]FULL

   Accepted, and changes nothing.

/RUNTIME

      /[NO]RUNTIME

   Accepted, and changes nothing.

/STATISTICS

      /[NO]STATISTICS

   Accepted, and changes nothing.

3 MODE

   Shows the display modes (SET MODE), and on a last line the CPU's
   access mode.

   Format

     SHOW MODE

3 MODULE

   Lists the current image's modules (see SET IMAGE): whether each is
   set, and the size of its symbol records.  MODULES is the same
   keyword.

   Format

     SHOW MODULE [module[,module...]] [/SHARE]

4 Parameter

 module

   The modules to list; * and % are wildcards.

4 Qualifier
/SHARE

   Adds a line for each of the other images.

3 OUTPUT

   Shows SET OUTPUT's settings: "noverify, terminal, noscreen_log,
   logging to DUA0:[WORK]DEBUG.LOG;1".

   Format

     SHOW OUTPUT

3 PAGE

   Shows the page table entry of an address (govax's own).  PTE is the
   same keyword.

   Format

     SHOW PAGE [/READ | /WRITE] address

4 Parameter

 address

   An address in the page: an expression.

4 Qualifiers
/READ

   Checks the entry for a read.

/WRITE

   Checks the entry for a write.

4 Description

   The entry's fields are shown, its protection by name (PTE$K_KW), and
   its form: valid; or, when not valid, a process section page (its
   PSTX), a global page (its GPTX), a page in the page file (its block),
   a page in transition on the free or modified list (its frame), or
   demand zero.

3 PROCESS

   Lists the processes: each one's PID, name, scheduling state (as SHOW
   SYSTEM shows it), and PC, the visible one (see SET PROCESS) marked
   with "*".  PROCESSES is the same keyword.

   Format

     SHOW PROCESS

3 PSL

   Shows the processor status longword, as a number and as its fields
   (govax's own).

   Format

     SHOW PSL

3 QUANTUM

   Shows the quantum (SET QUANTUM) the machine started with and its
   current count, and whether the host's clock drives the time (govax's
   own).

   Format

     SHOW QUANTUM

3 RADIX

   Shows the input and output radix.

   Format

     SHOW RADIX

3 REGIONS

   Shows the limits of the P0, P1, and S0 regions image activation uses
   (govax's own).

   Format

     SHOW REGIONS

3 REGISTERS

   Shows the general registers and the PSL (govax's own).  REG is the
   same keyword.  A single register is SHOW and its name.

   Format

     SHOW REGISTERS

3 SCB

   Shows the system control block, which holds the address of each
   exception's and interrupt's routine (govax's own).

   Format

     SHOW SCB [/ALL]

4 Qualifier
/ALL

      /[NO]ALL

   Every vector, not only those in use.

3 SCOPE

   Shows the scope list.  The default list is every call level,
   innermost (level 0) first, the current one marked with an asterisk,
   each with the routine there; a routine entered more than once is
   numbered (FACT 1).  A SET SCOPE list is shown as it was given.

   Format

     SHOW SCOPE

3 SHIM

   Shows the shims: the stubs that stand for shareable images' routines,
   and what each reaches, govax's own code or a microkernel routine
   (govax's own).

   Format

     SHOW SHIM

3 SOURCE

   Lists the directories SET SOURCE named.

   Format

     SHOW SOURCE

3 SP

   Shows the longwords on the current stack (govax's own).  ESP, ISP,
   KSP, SSP, and USP show a mode's stack, or the interrupt stack.

   Format

     SHOW SP [count] [/ALL]

4 Parameter

 count

   How many longwords, in the input radix.

4 Qualifier
/ALL

      /[NO]ALL

   The whole stack.

3 SSP

   Shows the longwords on the supervisor mode stack (govax's own).  See
   SP.

   Format

     SHOW SSP [count] [/ALL]

3 STACK

   Describes each call frame in full: the saved registers, the argument
   count, and the condition handler.  govax's dump of a stack's
   longwords is SHOW SP.

   Format

     SHOW STACK [count]

3 STEP

   Shows what STEP does when it isn't told (SET STEP).

   Format

     SHOW STEP

3 STRING_POOL

   Shows the console's string pool: its base, size, and the next free
   place (govax's own).  The pool is the S0 memory where the interactive
   assembler (ASM) keeps the string constants its statements use: a
   quoted string where an address belongs becomes a descriptor in the
   pool, and its address is the operand's value.  STRINGS is the same
   keyword, and CANCEL STRINGS empties the pool.

   Format

     SHOW STRING_POOL

3 SYMBOLS

   Lists the symbols matching a pattern.

   Format

     SHOW SYMBOLS [/ADDRESS] [/TYPE] pattern [IN module[,module...]]
     SHOW SYMBOLS/ALL [pattern]
     SHOW SYMBOLS/SYSTEM [pattern]

4 Parameter

 pattern

   A name, with * and % as wildcards, matched with each symbol's name,
   or with its whole path name if the pattern has a backslash.

4 Qualifiers
/ADDRESS

      /[NO]ADDRESS

   Shows each symbol's address.

/ALL

   govax's own: lists the machine's symbol table alone, every symbol.

/SYSTEM

   govax's own: lists the machine's symbol table's system symbols alone
   (SHOW SYMBOLS/SYSTEM CONSOLE$*).

/TYPE

      /[NO]TYPE

   Shows each symbol's data type.

4 Description

   The program's symbols are listed routines first, then data and
   labels, then program sections, each by name.  A pattern that matches
   none of the program's is tried in the machine's symbol table (the
   symbols ASM defines, and the system's, such as PTE$K_UR), and then is
   %DEBUG-E-NOSYMBOL.  The console's SHOW SYMBOL shows DCL symbols, which
   are another table.

3 TB

   Shows the translation buffer: its hit and miss counts, and each entry
   in use (govax's own).  Flushes counts the times the whole buffer was
   emptied (MTPR to TBIA), and PFlushes the times only its process
   entries were, as LDPCTX does.  TRANSLATION_BUFFER is the same
   keyword.

   Format

     SHOW TB

3 TRACE

   Lists the tracepoints.  TRACEPOINTS is the same keyword.

   Format

     SHOW TRACE

3 TYPE

   Names SET TYPE's type for locations no program symbol types, or with
   /OVERRIDE the type SET TYPE/OVERRIDE gives every location ("none"
   without one).

   Format

     SHOW TYPE [/OVERRIDE]

4 Qualifier
/OVERRIDE

   The override type.

3 USP

   Shows the longwords on the user mode stack (govax's own).  See SP.

   Format

     SHOW USP [count] [/ALL]

3 WATCH

   Lists the watchpoints.  WATCHPOINTS is the same keyword.

   Format

     SHOW WATCH

2 STEP

   Runs the program a step, or several, and stops again.  A step is a
   source line or an instruction.

   Format

     STEP[/qualifier...] [count]

3 Parameter

 count

   How many steps to take (one if none is given); only where the last
   one ends is reported.

3 Qualifiers
/BRANCH

   Runs until a branch instruction is about to run.

/CALL

   Runs until a call instruction is about to run.

/IN

   The same as /INTO.

/INSTRUCTION

   A step is one instruction.

/INTO

   At a call, the step follows the call into the routine.

/LINE

   A step goes to the first instruction of the next source line (the
   default).  Where the program has no line numbers, a step is an
   instruction either way.

/OVER

   A call (CALLS, CALLG, JSB, BSBB, BSBW, or a change-mode instruction)
   runs to its end, and the step stops after it (the default).

/RETURN

   Runs until the RET that ends the routine's call frame is about to
   run.  The wait belongs to that frame: a deeper call's RET doesn't end
   it, and it lasts across breakpoints and other STEPs until the RET is
   reached.

/SILENT

      /[NO]SILENT

   /SILENT reports nothing; /NOSILENT (the default) reports where the
   step stopped.

/SOURCE

      /[NO]SOURCE

   Whether the source line is shown with the report.

3 Description

   A qualifier left out is what SET STEP chose.  Of /INTO, /OVER, and
   /RETURN the last one typed wins.  S and ST are the same command.

   A program may trace itself (PSL<T> set, its condition handler taking
   SS$_TBIT after each instruction).  As the VMS debugger does, a
   breakpoint takes the trace due where it stops, and going on from a
   breakpoint or a step, by STEP or GO, clears T after the first
   instruction, so the program's tracing ends there.  A breakpoint in
   the program's trace handler, or SET BREAK/EXCEPTION, leaves the
   tracing as it is.

3 Examples

   1.DBG> STEP

     Runs to the next source line, over any call.

   2.DBG> STEP/INSTRUCTION/INTO 3

     Runs three instructions, into any routine called.

2 SYMBOLIZE

   Shows each name the debugger has for an address.

   Format

     SYMBOLIZE address

   The program's routines, labels, and lines come first, then the names
   in the machine's symbol table.  An address with no name is shown as
   itself.

     DBG> SYMBOLIZE 5A0

2 TYPE

   Shows lines of a module's source file.

   Format

     TYPE [module\]line[:line][,...]

   Without a module's name the lines are the module's the program is
   in.  The numbers are read in the input radix.  A FORTRAN program's
   source file is the MACRO file the compiler wrote, which holds the
   FORTRAN lines as comments.
